    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69226: A 15-year-old girl with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64093: A 22-year-old girl presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28443: A 55-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18949: A 72-year-old boy presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30045: A 19-year-old male presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88195: A 62-year-old girl presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10110: A 55-year-old man presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Inverse psoriasis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis", "Irritant contact dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79057: A 38-year-old boy presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Demodex folliculorum", "Malassezia yeast species", "Microsporum canis", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22139: A 28-year-old girl with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30554: An 82-year-old female presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Stasis dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60508: A 19-year-old boy presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #33002: A 55-year-old boy with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Auspitz sign", "Nikolsky sign", "Darier sign", "Asboe-Hansen sign", "Koebner phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63114: A 34-year-old girl presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Malassezia furfur", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38397: A 55-year-old girl presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Candidal intertrigo", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Erythrasma", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Hailey-Hailey disease"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39544: A 62-year-old girl with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38624: A 45-year-old man in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)", "Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22247: A 17-year-old boy with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24232: A 15-year-old man is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-B27", "HLA-DR3", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-Cw6"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44035: A 17-year-old male with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #33610: A 15-year-old girl presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68418: A 22-year-old male presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10233: A 72-year-old man with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86248: A 38-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89295: A 22-year-old boy with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21201: A 62-year-old woman presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #99885: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Isotretinoin", "Spironolactone", "Metformin", "Doxycycline", "Finasteride"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18724: A 34-year-old man presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic dermatitis", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94103: A 19-year-old man presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32157: A 34-year-old boy presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40981: A 55-year-old boy with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Oral Valacyclovir", "Oral Isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50049: A 17-year-old girl presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Steroid-induced rosacea", "Tinea incognito", "Demodex folliculitis", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Pustular psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41577: A 78-year-old woman presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31662: A 68-year-old male with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63921: A 72-year-old female presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis fungoides", "Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85154: A 72-year-old male with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35570: A 38-year-old male with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82620: A 19-year-old boy presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Acne conglobata", "Furunculosis", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #54527: A 62-year-old man presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53925: A 50-year-old man with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16481: A 20-year-old boy presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61111: A 72-year-old female with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74554: A 17-year-old male presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64887: A 22-year-old girl presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63791: A 38-year-old boy presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79477: A 55-year-old male presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68426: A 55-year-old man presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16316: A 22-year-old female presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Irritant contact dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80143: A 55-year-old female presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Microsporum canis", "Demodex folliculorum", "Malassezia yeast species", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Staphylococcus epidermidis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47749: A 20-year-old male with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38700: An 82-year-old boy presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Stasis dermatitis", "Plaque psoriasis", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98634: A 19-year-old boy presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #30737: A 62-year-old girl with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Koebner phenomenon", "Nikolsky sign", "Darier sign", "Asboe-Hansen sign", "Auspitz sign"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74505: A 15-year-old man presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Malassezia furfur", "Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15610: A 22-year-old woman presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic dermatitis", "Hailey-Hailey disease", "Erythrasma", "Candidal intertrigo", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90973: A 20-year-old female with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22850: A 68-year-old man in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)", "Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Prurigo of pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78586: A 19-year-old man with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64539: A 22-year-old girl is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-DR3", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-B27", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-Cw6"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68556: A 20-year-old female with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40213: A 45-year-old female presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #17517: A 38-year-old man presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78253: A 50-year-old male with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70290: A 68-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50696: A 72-year-old girl with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37590: A 72-year-old male presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Papulopustular rosacea", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61635: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Spironolactone", "Finasteride", "Doxycycline", "Metformin", "Isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #81614: A 22-year-old male presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Papulopustular rosacea", "Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #14520: A 68-year-old female presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66362: A 38-year-old boy presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84491: A 72-year-old male with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Oral Isotretinoin", "Oral Valacyclovir", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79510: A 55-year-old boy presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Atopic dermatitis flare", "Demodex folliculitis", "Pustular psoriasis", "Tinea incognito", "Steroid-induced rosacea"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35478: A 34-year-old man presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85586: A 45-year-old boy with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium", "Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57890: A 34-year-old man presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen simplex chronicus", "Lichen planus", "Asteatotic eczema", "Plaque psoriasis", "Mycosis fungoides"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28112: A 78-year-old girl with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11528: A 68-year-old female with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #14794: A 72-year-old male presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Acne conglobata", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Furunculosis", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23082: A 68-year-old man presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15132: A 62-year-old man with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28770: A 22-year-old male presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution", "Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95284: A 78-year-old male with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41428: A 55-year-old woman presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #61985: A 34-year-old girl presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82709: A 28-year-old male presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74160: A 34-year-old boy presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22805: A 50-year-old man presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37507: A 15-year-old female presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Irritant contact dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59579: A 62-year-old girl presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Malassezia yeast species", "Demodex folliculorum", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Microsporum canis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74800: A 68-year-old female with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39670: An 82-year-old male presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Lichen simplex chronicus", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Stasis dermatitis", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82190: A 38-year-old male presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74500: A 22-year-old girl with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Asboe-Hansen sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Auspitz sign", "Nikolsky sign", "Darier sign"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80777: A 19-year-old male presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Malassezia furfur"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71595: A 62-year-old boy presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythrasma", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Hailey-Hailey disease", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Candidal intertrigo"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46080: A 15-year-old girl with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91840: A 19-year-old male in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94414: A 55-year-old man with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15278: A 28-year-old female is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-DR4", "HLA-DR3", "HLA-B27", "HLA-Cw6", "HLA-DQ2"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31582: A 34-year-old female with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #96736: A 15-year-old male presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #26741: A 19-year-old girl presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32803: A 34-year-old woman with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #19392: A 62-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85034: A 17-year-old girl with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64603: A 20-year-old boy presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hot tub folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Papulopustular rosacea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87961: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Spironolactone", "Metformin", "Finasteride", "Isotretinoin", "Doxycycline"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68717: A 20-year-old boy presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Seborrheic dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72011: A 38-year-old girl presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21461: A 19-year-old female presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74765: A 45-year-old woman with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Oral Isotretinoin", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Oral Valacyclovir"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82215: A 45-year-old male presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pustular psoriasis", "Tinea incognito", "Demodex folliculitis", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Steroid-induced rosacea"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21205: A 28-year-old male presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #97581: A 38-year-old woman with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #48909: A 34-year-old woman presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Mycosis fungoides", "Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #73023: A 34-year-old woman with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95966: A 19-year-old female with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27477: A 55-year-old boy presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Furunculosis", "Acne conglobata", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84941: A 38-year-old girl presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78513: A 50-year-old man with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39583: A 22-year-old man presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #83933: A 45-year-old female with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75292: A 50-year-old male presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92604: A 45-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13169: A 55-year-old male presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45163: A 17-year-old boy presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50642: A 50-year-old male presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53599: A 55-year-old girl presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Irritant contact dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93331: A 72-year-old man presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Malassezia yeast species", "Microsporum canis", "Demodex folliculorum", "Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80155: A 28-year-old male with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56531: An 82-year-old boy presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Stasis dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #37036: A 34-year-old woman presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #49805: A 55-year-old woman with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Asboe-Hansen sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Auspitz sign", "Nikolsky sign", "Darier sign"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88330: A 50-year-old girl presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Malassezia furfur"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27776: A 72-year-old girl presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Candidal intertrigo", "Hailey-Hailey disease", "Erythrasma", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47275: A 15-year-old woman with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #58073: A 55-year-old female in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10792: A 28-year-old man with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94990: A 19-year-old male is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-Cw6", "HLA-DR3", "HLA-B27", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-DR4"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80189: A 45-year-old female with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #99120: A 19-year-old female presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #74990: A 72-year-old boy presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation", "It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #44227: A 50-year-old female with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #49529: A 72-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #91546: A 68-year-old woman with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10863: A 78-year-old female presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15221: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Finasteride", "Isotretinoin", "Metformin", "Doxycycline", "Spironolactone"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94148: A 22-year-old male presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #55543: A 17-year-old male presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62693: A 20-year-old male presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41283: A 19-year-old female with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Oral Valacyclovir", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Oral Isotretinoin", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24774: A 68-year-old girl presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Demodex folliculitis", "Tinea incognito", "Steroid-induced rosacea", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Pustular psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16564: A 15-year-old boy presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #33227: A 19-year-old male with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)", "Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84637: A 45-year-old boy presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis fungoides", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62841: A 15-year-old male with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38096: A 68-year-old girl with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #12708: A 34-year-old male presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)", "Furunculosis", "Acne conglobata", "Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #65609: A 22-year-old girl presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35743: A 28-year-old boy with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21053: A 15-year-old female presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22894: A 17-year-old boy with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98113: A 34-year-old male presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98900: A 45-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45880: A 19-year-old girl presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95673: A 19-year-old man presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22728: A 68-year-old woman presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70578: A 38-year-old girl presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Irritant contact dermatitis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69245: A 78-year-old female presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Microsporum canis", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Demodex folliculorum", "Malassezia yeast species", "Staphylococcus epidermidis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32693: A 68-year-old woman with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24849: An 82-year-old girl presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Stasis dermatitis", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Plaque psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25603: A 78-year-old man presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56536: A 38-year-old girl with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Nikolsky sign", "Asboe-Hansen sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Auspitz sign", "Darier sign"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34140: A 22-year-old female presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Malassezia furfur"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22643: A 22-year-old girl presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythrasma", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Candidal intertrigo", "Hailey-Hailey disease"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #18586: A 78-year-old woman with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57501: A 72-year-old female in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35136: A 50-year-old woman with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60935: A 68-year-old male is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-B27", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-Cw6", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-DR3"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63876: A 68-year-old woman with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35954: A 50-year-old girl presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87928: A 20-year-old woman presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88346: A 72-year-old female with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88927: A 38-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32732: A 78-year-old male with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56648: A 22-year-old male presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84116: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Doxycycline", "Isotretinoin", "Metformin", "Finasteride", "Spironolactone"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90860: A 17-year-old male presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Papulopustular rosacea"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20510: A 72-year-old girl presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #45057: A 45-year-old woman presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70415: A 78-year-old boy with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Oral Isotretinoin", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Oral Valacyclovir"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #58243: A 19-year-old female presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Tinea incognito", "Steroid-induced rosacea", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Demodex folliculitis", "Pustular psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #69155: A 20-year-old boy presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86793: A 38-year-old girl with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80458: A 15-year-old male presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis fungoides", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Lichen planus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #41846: A 45-year-old girl with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #73326: A 62-year-old girl with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53405: A 78-year-old male presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Acne conglobata", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)", "Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Furunculosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27908: A 62-year-old boy presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43903: A 28-year-old girl with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11283: A 45-year-old male presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46059: A 72-year-old girl with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76506: A 78-year-old girl presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63532: A 20-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70966: A 34-year-old male presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34722: A 50-year-old man presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35035: A 45-year-old female presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #52640: A 45-year-old boy presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Irritant contact dermatitis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #42876: A 55-year-old woman presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Microsporum canis", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Demodex folliculorum", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Malassezia yeast species"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50998: A 72-year-old girl with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #97267: An 82-year-old girl presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Stasis dermatitis", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #77467: A 62-year-old male presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84248: A 50-year-old man with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Auspitz sign", "Asboe-Hansen sign", "Nikolsky sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Darier sign"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71264: A 45-year-old man presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Malassezia furfur", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62779: A 55-year-old man presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Erythrasma", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Candidal intertrigo", "Hailey-Hailey disease"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #17167: A 17-year-old boy with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35113: A 34-year-old boy in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Pemphigoid gestationis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80228: A 15-year-old girl with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72659: A 72-year-old girl is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-B27", "HLA-DR3", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-Cw6"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #73217: A 45-year-old boy with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43746: A 15-year-old man presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #83308: A 20-year-old male presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76183: A 72-year-old woman with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80725: A 28-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #57982: A 68-year-old male with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #55226: A 50-year-old woman presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95483: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Spironolactone", "Doxycycline", "Finasteride", "Isotretinoin", "Metformin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #82728: A 45-year-old woman presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34521: A 38-year-old male presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43188: A 22-year-old female presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62906: A 17-year-old male with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Oral Isotretinoin", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Oral Valacyclovir", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #25643: A 22-year-old woman presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Steroid-induced rosacea", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Tinea incognito", "Pustular psoriasis", "Demodex folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #83979: A 15-year-old female presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #56684: A 17-year-old male with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #98248: A 28-year-old woman presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Mycosis fungoides"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20801: A 34-year-old female with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23334: A 28-year-old girl with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #89409: A 19-year-old woman presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne conglobata", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)", "Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Furunculosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #80061: A 72-year-old female presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #84430: A 38-year-old girl with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #43433: A 20-year-old man presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75137: A 17-year-old boy with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40185: A 38-year-old boy presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23407: A 72-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46827: A 50-year-old girl presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39692: A 62-year-old male presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #99296: A 28-year-old man presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53350: A 72-year-old woman presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Irritant contact dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88225: A 34-year-old girl presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Microsporum canis", "Malassezia yeast species", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Demodex folliculorum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60858: A 15-year-old male with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #17570: An 82-year-old female presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stasis dermatitis", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Lichen simplex chronicus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22662: A 62-year-old woman presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31464: A 78-year-old male with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Nikolsky sign", "Auspitz sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Darier sign", "Asboe-Hansen sign"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #63106: A 19-year-old man presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Malassezia furfur"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #12018: A 34-year-old boy presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Candidal intertrigo", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Hailey-Hailey disease", "Erythrasma", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #88265: A 19-year-old male with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68732: A 20-year-old boy in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34037: A 38-year-old female with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39904: A 45-year-old male is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-DQ2", "HLA-B27", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-Cw6", "HLA-DR3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86008: A 72-year-old woman with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93312: A 15-year-old man presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68117: A 22-year-old woman presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #72264: A 38-year-old boy with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75921: A 72-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90922: A 20-year-old girl with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92572: A 19-year-old male presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #85189: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Finasteride", "Doxycycline", "Metformin", "Spironolactone", "Isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23976: A 72-year-old woman presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Seborrheic dermatitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Carcinoid syndrome flushing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40520: A 50-year-old girl presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #49427: A 22-year-old boy presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27297: A 38-year-old woman with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Oral Isotretinoin", "Oral Valacyclovir", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76164: A 17-year-old woman presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Atopic dermatitis flare", "Steroid-induced rosacea", "Demodex folliculitis", "Tinea incognito", "Pustular psoriasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28745: A 72-year-old girl presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90817: A 55-year-old boy with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)", "Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #16526: A 17-year-old female presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Asteatotic eczema", "Plaque psoriasis", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Mycosis fungoides"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21064: A 22-year-old woman with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #71293: A 28-year-old female with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40354: A 28-year-old boy presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Acne conglobata", "Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)", "Furunculosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #62707: A 62-year-old boy presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows", "Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27239: A 62-year-old girl with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59214: A 17-year-old boy presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Topical salicylic acid 10% solution", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #78859: A 72-year-old girl with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone", "Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38414: A 68-year-old female presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64175: A 28-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks", "Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #28363: A 55-year-old man presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31381: A 68-year-old boy presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #70579: A 17-year-old male presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE", "Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #38724: A 38-year-old male presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Inverse psoriasis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis", "Irritant contact dermatitis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13308: A 28-year-old woman presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Malassezia yeast species", "Demodex folliculorum", "Cutibacterium acnes", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Microsporum canis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66043: A 62-year-old male with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13135: An 82-year-old girl presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Plaque psoriasis", "Stasis dermatitis", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50292: A 38-year-old man presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76107: A 19-year-old man with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Asboe-Hansen sign", "Auspitz sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Darier sign", "Nikolsky sign"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53137: A 55-year-old woman presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Malassezia furfur", "Epstein-Barr Virus", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #95886: A 19-year-old female presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Candidal intertrigo", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Hailey-Hailey disease", "Erythrasma"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94692: A 50-year-old female with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90228: A 55-year-old female in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Prurigo of pregnancy", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #83462: A 50-year-old male with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #94738: A 68-year-old boy is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-DR3", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-B27", "HLA-DQ2", "HLA-Cw6"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #13361: A 28-year-old boy with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10782: A 72-year-old man presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release", "Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #47653: A 17-year-old man presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #93390: A 34-year-old female with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway", "Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #90367: A 68-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #34838: A 19-year-old female with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #12309: A 55-year-old woman presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27209: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Spironolactone", "Doxycycline", "Finasteride", "Metformin", "Isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #24706: A 19-year-old woman presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #76249: A 78-year-old woman presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel", "Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #20980: A 45-year-old female presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #59938: A 34-year-old man with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Oral Isotretinoin", "Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Oral Valacyclovir", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #39973: A 38-year-old man presents with a facial rash that has been present for 3 months. The patient was initially diagnosed with eczema and treated with high-potency topical betamethasone dipropionate cream daily. The facial redness has progressively worsened and is now studded with hundreds of tiny pustules and telangiectasias. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pustular psoriasis", "Demodex folliculitis", "Atopic dermatitis flare", "Steroid-induced rosacea", "Tinea incognito"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Steroid-induced rosacea (or steroid rosacea) is a classic complication of applying potent topical corticosteroids to the face. The steroids cause initial vasoconstriction but eventually lead to rebound vasodilation, epidermal atrophy, and a severe pustular eruption upon withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #66689: A 17-year-old female presents with a highly pruritic rash on the face and neck. They note that the rash is made worse by wearing wool scarves. Physical exam shows ill-defined, scaly, erythematous plaques on the cheeks and neck with linear excoriations. What is the hallmark microscopic finding of this acute condition?', '["Kogoj spongiform pustules in the upper spinous layer", "Lichenoid interface dermatitis with colloid bodies", "Spongiosis (intercellular edema in the epidermis)", "Subepidermal deposition of IgA in the dermal papillae", "Acantholysis (loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clinical picture describes acute eczema (contact or atopic dermatitis). The hallmark histologic feature of acute eczematous dermatitis is spongiosis, which is intercellular edema within the epidermis that pushes keratinocytes apart, often forming intraepidermal vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #31019: A 19-year-old boy with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne is being treated with oral isotretinoin. Which of the following laboratory panels must be checked regularly during the course of this therapy?', '["Complete blood count with differential and serum uric acid", "Fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (ALT/AST)", "Hemoglobin A1c and serum amylase/lipase", "Basic metabolic panel and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Coagulation panel (PT/INR) and serum calcium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oral isotretinoin classically causes hypertriglyceridemia and elevations in transaminases (hepatotoxicity). Therefore, standard monitoring guidelines require obtaining a baseline fasting lipid panel and liver function tests (LFTs), with periodic repeat testing during the treatment course.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #75915: A 34-year-old woman presents with a chronic, severely pruritic, thickened patch of skin on their posterior neck. They admit to scratching it constantly due to anxiety. Physical exam shows a single, hyperpigmented, leathery plaque with deeply accentuated skin markings. Biopsy reveals hyperkeratosis and regular acanthosis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycosis fungoides", "Lichen planus", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Asteatotic eczema"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lichen simplex chronicus (circumscribed neurodermatitis) is a localized plaque of chronic eczematous dermatitis that develops solely in response to repetitive scratching or rubbing of a single area. This mechanical trauma induces reactive epidermal hyperplasia, leading to characteristic lichenification (leathery, thickened skin with accentuated markings).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11556: A 62-year-old male with a history of mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with a severe panic disorder. The physician prescribes a beta-blocker (propranolol) to assist with performance anxiety. Two weeks later, the patient presents with a massive, generalized worsening of their psoriatic plaques. Which of the following drugs is a well-known trigger for exacerbating psoriasis?', '["Amlodipine, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Metformin, Sulfonylureas, and Insulin", "Beta-blockers, Lithium, and Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine)", "Penicillin, Cephalosporins, and Macrolides", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Several systemic medications are well-documented triggers for severe exacerbations of psoriasis. These include beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), systemic corticosteroid withdrawal, and interferon-alpha.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15726: A 68-year-old male with moderate inflammatory acne has been treated with oral doxycycline for 3 months with good improvement. However, they present to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain on their face and forearms after spending only 15 minutes outdoors. What is the mechanism of this drug reaction?', '["Drug-induced systemic lupus erythematosus (DI-SLE) triggered by sun exposure", "Direct chemical burning of the epidermis by topically excreted doxycycline in sweat", "Type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction to sunlight proteins", "Autoimmune destruction of melanocytes by doxycycline-induced T-cells", "Phototoxicity induced by absorption of UV-A light by the tetracycline drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Tetracyclines (particularly doxycycline) are notorious causes of drug-induced phototoxicity. The drug molecules absorb UV-A radiation in the skin, releasing free radicals that damage surrounding cell membranes and DNA, presenting as an exaggerated sunburn reaction on sun-exposed areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #26874: A 15-year-old girl presents with a severe, highly inflammatory, and chronic skin condition characterized by double-headed comedones, painful deep nodules, and interconnecting sinus tracts discharging foul-smelling purulent fluid. The lesions are localized to the axillae, groin, and perineum. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hidradenitis suppurativa (Acne inversa)", "Furunculosis", "Crohn''s disease cutaneous fistulae", "Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp", "Acne conglobata"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the follicular pilosebaceous unit, classically localized to intertriginous apocrine-gland bearing skin (axillae, groin, perineum, inframammary). It presents with recurrent painful nodules, double-headed comedones, abscesses, sinus tracts, and scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #68497: A 78-year-old female presents with a persistent facial eruption. On examination, the patient has prominent malar erythema that spares the nasolabial folds, but is also noted to have scaly, erythematous plaques on their elbows. What clinical sign or feature would most strongly differentiate Plaque Psoriasis from Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) in this patient?', '["Psoriasis has a positive Nikolsky sign on physical exam", "The malar rash of SLE strictly involves the nasolabial folds", "SLE malar rash is highly pruritic and bleeds when scraped", "Psoriasis always presents with oral ulcers and joint pain", "Psoriatic plaques classically involve the elbows and have a silvery scale, while the SLE malar rash spares the nasolabial folds and elbows"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The malar (''butterfly'') rash of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus classically spares the nasolabial folds and is non-scaly. Psoriatic plaques classically affect the extensor surfaces (elbows/knees) and are covered in a dense, silvery-white scale, with a positive Auspitz sign.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #42565: A 15-year-old man with severe atopic dermatitis presents with a 24-hour history of severe skin itching and redness. They spent the previous afternoon clearing brush in their backyard. What morphological feature would help distinguish acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis from an acute atopic dermatitis flare?', '["The presence of honey-colored crusts on the extensor surfaces", "Lichenification and scaling over the flexural folds", "Symmetrical distribution over the cheeks and scalp", "Severe pruritus that is worse at night", "The presence of linear configurations of vesicles corresponding to plant contact"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (especially Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is characterized by a linear arrangement of papules and vesicles, indicating the direct linear brush of the offending plant resin (urushiol) against the skin. Atopic dermatitis is typically symmetrical and flexural without linear vesicles.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #92989: A 20-year-old boy presents with a severe flaking rash in the external auditory canals and behind the ears. The skin is erythematous with greasy, yellowish scaling. What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for this localized presentation?', '["Topical salicylic acid 10% solution", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 5% wash", "Systemic oral griseofulvin therapy for 4 weeks", "Topical mupirocin 2% ointment twice daily", "Topical ketoconazole 2% shampoo or cream"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Seborrheic dermatitis of the scalp and external auditory canals is driven by Malassezia yeast. The first-line targeted treatment is topical antifungal therapy (e.g., ketoconazole 2% cream or selenium sulfide/ketoconazole shampoos) to reduce yeast colonization, combined with low-potency steroids if highly inflamed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #42439: A 62-year-old man with a history of asthma and seasonal allergies presents with a severely pruritic rash on the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Physical exam shows erythematous, excoriated, and lichenified plaques. What is the primary pathophysiological defect responsible for this condition?', '["Type I hypersensitivity reaction to dietary gluten proteins", "Loss-of-function mutation in the filaggrin (FLG) gene leading to epidermal barrier dysfunction", "Autoimmune destruction of desmoglein 1 and 3 in the spinous layer", "T-cell mediated apoptosis of keratinocytes at the dermo-epidermal junction", "Defective synthesis of type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal barrier dysfunction, classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin (FLG) gene. This leads to increased transepidermal water loss, allergen penetration, and secondary T-helper 2 (Th2) mediated inflammation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #14947: A 78-year-old male presents with a rapid eruption of monomorphic, painful, punched-out, hemorrhagic erosions with overlying black crusts clustered over their face, neck, and upper chest. The patient has a background of severe atopic dermatitis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Recommend daily bleach baths and initiate oral antihistamines", "Apply high-potency topical clobetasol ointment under occlusion", "Initiate immediate systemic acyclovir therapy", "Prescribe oral cephalexin and apply topical mupirocin cream", "Perform a punch biopsy to rule out squamous cell carcinoma in situ"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This presentation is diagnostic of Eczema Herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption), a disseminated HSV infection overlying active atopic dermatitis. It is a dermatologic emergency requiring prompt systemic antiviral therapy (acyclovir) to prevent ophthalmic involvement or systemic viremia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #49935: A 38-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral lower legs during the winter. Physical exam reveals several sharply demarcated, coin-shaped (discoid), erythematous, scaly plaques. A KOH prep is negative for fungal elements. What is the first-line treatment of choice?', '["Topical salicylic acid 6% ointment to induce keratolysis", "Oral cephalexin for 7 days to treat secondary bacterial infection", "Mid-to-high potency topical corticosteroids and aggressive emollient moisturization", "Oral prednisone taper starting at 40 mg daily", "Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 2 weeks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nummular eczema (discoid eczema) classically presents as coin-shaped scaly plaques on the extremities of adults, often exacerbated by dry winter weather. The negative KOH rules out tinea corporis. First-line management consists of high-potency topical steroids and frequent use of thick emollients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35473: A 15-year-old man presents with recurrent, extremely pruritic, deep-seated, clear vesicles resembling ''tapioca pudding'' clustered along the lateral aspects of the fingers and palms. What is the most likely trigger or association for this patient''s condition?', '["Reactivation of latent varicella zoster virus in the dorsal root ganglion", "Emotional stress, hot weather, and frequent hand washing (wet work)", "Ingestion of high-gluten containing grain products", "Direct contact with toxicodendrol resin (urushiol) from poison ivy", "Staphylococcus aureus colonization of the hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dyshidrotic eczema (pompholyx) classically presents with deep-seated, intensely pruritic ''tapioca-like'' vesicles on the hands, lateral digits, or soles. It is not associated with sweat gland dysfunction, but rather triggered by stress, wet work, metal allergies (nickel/cobalt), and warm weather.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #53077: A 20-year-old man presents with a severe linear streak of intensely pruritic, erythematous papules and vesicles on their forearm 48 hours after hiking in the woods. What is the primary immune mediator responsible for this clinical presentation?', '["Complement-mediated antibody-directed cytotoxicity (Type II hypersensitivity)", "Mast cell degranulation via IgE cross-linking (Type I hypersensitivity)", "Polymorphonuclear leukocyte activation by bacterial superantigens", "Circulating antigen-antibody immune complexes (Type III hypersensitivity)", "Sensitized T-lymphocytes (Type IV hypersensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Allergic contact dermatitis (such as Rhus dermatitis from poison ivy/oak) is a classic Type IV (delayed-type, T-cell mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol acts as a hapten, binding to skin proteins which are processed by Langerhans cells and presented to T-cells.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #60083: A 45-year-old female presents with a well-demarcated, intensely itchy, scaly, and erythematous plaque located directly beneath their navel, corresponding exactly to where their jeans button touches the skin. What is the gold standard diagnostic test to confirm this patient''s suspected allergen?', '["Patch testing read at 48 and 96 hours", "Punch biopsy of the active border of the lesion", "Skin prick testing with immediate wheal-and-flare measurement", "Oral food allergen challenge under medical supervision", "Serum radioallergosorbent test (RAST) for allergen-specific IgE"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clinical scenario describes nickel-induced allergic contact dermatitis, very common at the infraumbilical zone from metal snaps/buttons. The gold standard diagnosis is patch testing, which evaluates Type IV delayed hypersensitivity by applying suspected allergens to the back for 48 hours.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23851: A 38-year-old man presents with a bright red, raw diaper rash. On physical examination, the inguinal creases are completely spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Inverse psoriasis", "Langerhans cell histiocytosis", "Seborrheic diaper dermatitis", "Irritant contact dermatitis", "Candidal diaper dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Irritant diaper dermatitis is caused by friction and prolonged contact with urine and feces, classically affecting the convex surfaces of the buttocks and thighs while sparing the deep inguinal skin folds (creases). In contrast, candidal diaper dermatitis characteristically involves the folds and presents with satellite pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21764: A 50-year-old male presents with an itchy, flaking rash on their face and scalp. Physical exam reveals ill-defined erythematous patches with fine, greasy, yellowish scales located on the nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. What organism is strongly implicated in the pathogenesis of this disease?', '["Cutibacterium acnes", "Microsporum canis", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Demodex folliculorum", "Malassezia yeast species"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Seborrheic dermatitis classically presents with greasy, yellowish scales on sebum-rich areas (scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, ears, pre-sternal chest). It is driven by an inflammatory response to Malassezia (formerly Pityrosporum) species, a lipophilic yeast colonizing these oily areas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #27431: A 45-year-old female with a history of obesity, multiple pregnancies, and varicose veins presents with bilateral pitting edema, reddish-brown hyperpigmentation (hemosiderin staining), and weeping scaly plaques over the medial malleoli. What is the most critical, foundational treatment to resolve this skin condition?', '["Surgical debridement of the medial malleolar plaques", "Systemic broad-spectrum oral antibiotics", "Long-term oral prednisone therapy", "Graduated compression therapy and leg elevation", "Ultra-high potency topical steroids under occlusion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has stasis dermatitis secondary to chronic venous insufficiency. Hydrostatic pressure leads to RBC extravasation, causing hemosiderin staining. The foundational, pathophysiologically targeted therapy is reversing venous hypertension using graduated compression stockings and leg elevation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #64404: An 82-year-old female presents with an intensely itchy rash on the bilateral anterior shins during mid-winter. Physical exam shows extremely dry skin with a network of fine fissures and cracks resembling a ''dried riverbed'' or ''cracked porcelain''. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stasis dermatitis", "Lichen simplex chronicus", "Plaque psoriasis", "Ichthyosis vulgaris", "Asteatotic eczema (Eczema craquel\u00e9)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Asteatotic eczema (eczema craquelé) is common in elderly individuals during winter. It is caused by a severe decrease in skin surface lipids, leading to dehydration and characteristically cracked skin on the shins, aggravated by frequent hot baths.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #21024: A 17-year-old girl presents with thick, erythematous plaques covered by dense, silvery-white, micaceous scales on the extensor surfaces of their elbows and knees. What histologic feature is classically seen in this condition?', '["Subepidermal split with a dense infiltrate of eosinophils in the dermal papillary tips", "Hyperkeratosis, granulomatous dermal infiltrate, and microabscesses of eosinophils", "Intraepidermal acantholysis with a ''tombstone'' appearance of the basal layer", "Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermo-epidermal junction with apoptotic keratinocytes (Civatte bodies)", "Acanthosis with elongated rete ridges, parakeratosis, and neutrophilic microabscesses in the stratum corneum"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Histology of plaque psoriasis classically demonstrates epidermal hyperplasia (acanthosis) with uniform elongation of the rete ridges, parakeratosis (retention of nuclei in the stratum corneum), thinning of the suprapapillary plates, and Munro microabscesses (neutrophils in the stratum corneum).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #10388: A 28-year-old woman with chronic plaque psoriasis scratches their elbow and, 2 weeks later, develops a new linear psoriatic plaque exactly corresponding to the scratch mark. What is this phenomenon called?', '["Asboe-Hansen sign", "Darier sign", "Auspitz sign", "Nikolsky sign", "Koebner phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Koebner phenomenon (isomorphic response) is the development of typical cutaneous lesions of a disease (like psoriasis, lichen planus, or vitiligo) at sites of localized trauma to previously normal skin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #36097: A 78-year-old male presents with a sudden, explosive eruption of hundreds of small, 2-10 mm, drop-like, scaly papules over their trunk and limbs. They report having a severe sore throat 2 weeks ago. What is the most likely pathogen associated with this eruption?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Malassezia furfur", "Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)", "Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1", "Epstein-Barr Virus"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Guttate psoriasis classically presents as a sudden eruption of small, drop-like, scaly lesions across the trunk and extremities, classically triggered 1-3 weeks following a Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcal pharyngitis or tonsillitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #35230: A 28-year-old woman presents with bright red, highly demarcated, completely smooth, non-scaly, and macerated plaques in the bilateral axillae, inframammary folds, and inguinal creases. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hailey-Hailey disease", "Inverse (flexural) psoriasis", "Erythrasma", "Candidal intertrigo", "Seborrheic dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inverse psoriasis (flexural psoriasis) selectively targets intertriginous areas (axillae, groin, inframammary folds, gluteal cleft). Because of the warm, moist environment, the classic silvery-white scaling is macerated and absent, leaving smooth, highly erythematous, glazed plaques.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #23251: A 34-year-old boy with a history of plaque psoriasis presents to the emergency department with fever, severe chills, and generalized burning erythema covering 95% of their total body surface area. What is the most immediate life-threatening complication of this condition?', '["Acute kidney injury from myoglobinuria", "High-output cardiac failure and thermoregulatory dysfunction", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Anaphylactic shock from endogenous histamine release", "Severe autoimmune thrombocytopenic purpura"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythrodermic psoriasis is a dermatologic emergency where >90% of the body surface area is erythematous and scaly. The loss of skin barrier leads to massive vasodilation, causing hypothermia, high-output heart failure (due to shunting of blood to the skin), and massive fluid/protein loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #17370: A 55-year-old woman in their 3rd trimester of pregnancy presents with fever, malaise, and a sudden eruption of widespread sheets of painful erythema studded with hundreds of small, sterile, coalescing yellow pustules. What is this condition historically called?', '["Subcorneal pustular dermatosis (Sneddon-Wilkinson)", "Pemphigoid gestationis", "Impetigo herpetiformis (Generalized Pustular Psoriasis of Pregnancy)", "Prurigo of pregnancy", "Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy (PUPPP)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Impetigo herpetiformis is a rare, severe variant of generalized pustular psoriasis occurring during pregnancy, typically in the third trimester. It is characterized by sterile pustules, fever, leukocytosis, and hypocalcemia, and poses significant risks for placental insufficiency and fetal demise.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #50947: A 17-year-old man with a history of psoriasis presents to the clinic with asymmetric joint pain and swelling. Physical exam reveals sausage-like swelling of the entire right index finger and left second toe. What radiographic finding is classically seen in this condition?', '["Symmetrical joint space narrowing with periarticular osteopenia", "Bilateral subluxation of the metacarpophalangeal joints", "Chondrocalcinosis of the articular cartilage", "''Pencil-in-cup'' deformity of the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints", "Punched-out bony erosions with overhanging margins (rat-bite erosions)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The patient has psoriatic arthritis presenting with dactylitis (''sausage digit''). The classic radiographic finding of advanced psoriatic arthritis, particularly involving the DIP joints, is the ''pencil-in-cup'' deformity, reflecting osteolysis combined with adjacent bony proliferation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87200: A 72-year-old female is concerned about progressive fingernail changes. Examination shows multiple small, ice-pick-like depressions on the surface of the nail plates, along with yellowish-brown spots resembling ''oil drops'' underneath the nail plates. Which HLA subtype is most strongly linked to this disease?', '["HLA-DQ2", "HLA-DR4", "HLA-B27", "HLA-Cw6", "HLA-DR3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nail pitting and the ''oil drop'' sign are pathognomonic manifestations of psoriatic nail disease. Psoriasis is strongly linked to the major histocompatibility complex region, specifically the HLA-Cw6 allele, which is associated with early-onset plaque psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #42178: A 17-year-old man with severe, refractory plaque psoriasis is being evaluated for systemic biologic therapy. A purified protein derivative (PPD) skin test is positive (15 mm induration), but chest X-ray shows no active infiltrates. What is the most appropriate next step before initiating a TNF-alpha inhibitor?', '["Prescribe oral prednisone to suppress the PPD hypersensitivity reaction", "Administer a BCG vaccine and retest in 3 months", "Proceed with starting the TNF-alpha inhibitor immediately since the chest X-ray is normal", "Initiate treatment for latent tuberculosis infection and wait at least 1 month before starting the biologic", "Start a 4-drug active TB regimen (RIPE) and delay the biologic for 6 months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Before starting any TNF-alpha inhibitor (e.g., adalimumab, infliximab), patients must be screened for latent TB. If positive, treatment for latent TB (typically isoniazid) must be initiated, and the biologic should be delayed for at least 1 month of anti-TB therapy to prevent catastrophic reactivation of TB.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #40598: A 72-year-old man presents with numerous blackheads (open comedones) and whiteheads (closed comedones) on their forehead and nose. There is no erythema, inflammatory papules, or cysts. What is the primary mechanism of action of the first-line topical therapy for this patient?', '["Reversible binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit to inhibit protein synthesis", "Normalization of follicular keratinization and promotion of comedolysis", "Inhibition of the 5-alpha-reductase enzyme in sebaceous glands", "Competitive antagonism of androgen receptors on the sebocyte membrane", "Direct bactericidal activity against Cutibacterium acnes via free radical release"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Comedonal acne is treated first-line with topical retinoids (e.g., tretinoin, adapalene). Retinoids function by binding to nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RARs) to normalize follicular desquamation, preventing keratinocyte hyperadhesion and facilitating comedolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #86317: A 55-year-old girl presents with moderate papulopustular acne. The dermatologist prescribes a combination of topical clindamycin and topical benzoyl peroxide. What is the primary clinical rationale for including benzoyl peroxide in this regimen?', '["It selectively blocks systemic absorption of clindamycin to avoid pseudomembranous colitis", "It acts as a local anesthetic to reduce the burning sensation of topical retinoids", "It enhances the absorption of clindamycin by digesting sebum lipids", "It acts as a chemical sunscreen to prevent post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation", "It is highly bactericidal and prevents the emergence of clindamycin-resistant Cutibacterium acnes strains"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Monotherapy with topical antibiotics for acne leads to rapid bacterial resistance. Benzoyl peroxide is a potent non-antibiotic antimicrobial that releases singlet oxygen free radicals, killing C. acnes with zero risk of resistance. Combining it with clindamycin prevents the development of resistant bacterial strains.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #96772: A 34-year-old woman with severe, scarring nodulocystic acne has failed oral minocycline and multiple topical regimens. The physician decides to start oral isotretinoin. What is the absolute molecular mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Upregulation of Th2-cytokines to suppress neutrophil chemotaxis", "Direct bactericidal lysis of the cell wall of Cutibacterium acnes", "Direct competitive inhibition of the androgen receptor on the sebaceous gland", "Induction of apoptosis in sebocytes, leading to profound reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum excretion", "Irreversible inhibition of the cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inflammatory pathway"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oral isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is the most effective acne treatment because it targets all four pathogenic factors. Its primary molecular mechanism is inducing apoptosis in sebaceous gland cells, leading to a massive (up to 90%) reduction in sebaceous gland size and sebum output.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #22861: A 45-year-old female of childbearing potential is starting oral isotretinoin. Under the iPLEDGE program, what is the absolute requirement for her to receive her monthly prescription?', '["Sign a waiver releasing the manufacturer from liability and undergo weekly urine HCG tests", "Complete a monthly survey confirming abstinence and have a negative urine pregnancy test every 3 months", "Commit to using a single high-dose copper IUD and obtain bi-monthly blood pregnancy tests", "Have a documented bilateral tubal ligation or partner vasectomy", "Use two independent, effective forms of contraception simultaneously and obtain a negative monthly pregnancy test"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Due to the extreme teratogenicity of isotretinoin (causing severe craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations), the iPLEDGE program in the US mandates that all females of childbearing potential use two simultaneous forms of contraception and obtain a negative pregnancy test monthly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #11039: A 62-year-old female with a history of severe nodulocystic acne presents to the emergency room with a 5-day history of high fever, joint pain (polyarthralgia), and an acute, painful, ulcerating eruption of necrotic plaques with hemorrhagic crusts on their back and chest. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for this acute condition?', '["High-dose immediate oral isotretinoin (1.0 mg/kg/day)", "Broadband UVB phototherapy and topical high-potency corticosteroids", "Immediate discontinuation of all medications and wet-to-dry dressings", "Intravenous broad-spectrum cephalosporins and surgical debridement", "Systemic corticosteroids followed by cautious, low-dose oral isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The patient has Acne Fulminans, an immunologically mediated systemic reaction to C. acnes antigens. It presents with acute, ulcerating, hemorrhagic acne lesions accompanied by systemic symptoms (fever, osteolytic bone lesions, polyarthralgias). First-line treatment requires systemic corticosteroids to calm the inflammatory storm, followed by slow introduction of low-dose isotretinoin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #87569: A 28-year-old male presents with a sudden onset of monomorphic, erythematous papules and pustules on their chest and upper back. The patient denies having blackheads or whiteheads. They were recently treated with a 3-week course of high-dose oral prednisone for an asthma flare. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Acne vulgaris (nodulocystic subtype)", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Steroid-induced acne (or steroid folliculitis) is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules typically distributed on the trunk. Key differentiating features from acne vulgaris are the uniform stage of all lesions (monomorphic) and the complete absence of comedones.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #97127: A 26-year-old female presents with persistent, painful acne nodules localized strictly to her jawline and chin, which worsen significantly during the week prior to her menses. She also reports irregular periods and recent male-pattern hair growth on her face. What is the most effective hormone-targeted oral therapy for her acne?', '["Doxycycline", "Metformin", "Finasteride", "Spironolactone", "Isotretinoin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has adult female hormonal acne, highly correlated with hyperandrogenism (likely PCOS). The first-line, highly effective hormone therapy is spironolactone, which acts as a potassium-sparing diuretic and a competitive androgen receptor antagonist, blocking testosterone at the sebaceous gland.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #46503: A 55-year-old man presents with a persistent facial redness. They note that drinking hot coffee, eating spicy foods, or going out in the sun causes intense, burning facial flushing. Physical exam shows persistent erythema and telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, but NO comedones, papules, or pustules. What is the diagnosis?', '["Carcinoid syndrome flushing", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (malar rash)", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea is the vascular subtype of rosacea. It is characterized by persistent central facial erythema, flushing triggered by hot beverages, alcohol, spicy foods, or temperature changes, and prominent telangiectasias, without inflammatory papules/pustules.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #32567: A 55-year-old man presents with central facial flushing and persistent redness associated with numerous inflammatory papules and pustules on the nose and cheeks. There are no comedones. What is the first-line topical treatment of choice for this patient''s condition?', '["Topical hydrocortisone 2.5% ointment", "Topical benzoyl peroxide 10% wash", "Topical salicylic acid 2% solution", "Topical metronidazole cream or topical ivermectin", "Topical clindamycin 1% gel"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Papulopustular rosacea presents with central facial erythema and inflammatory papules/pustules, lacking the comedones of acne. First-line topical treatments include metronidazole, ivermectin, or azelaic acid, which have anti-inflammatory and antiparasitic properties.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #15975: A 62-year-old girl presents with a bulbous, grossly hypertrophic, and irregular enlargement of their nose with prominent, dilated pores. The patient states they have suffered from ''red face'' for decades. What is the underlying pathophysiology of this condition?', '["Autoimmune destruction of the nasal septal cartilage (relapsing polychondritis)", "Chronic deep subcutaneous Staphylococcal infection of the nasal cartilage", "Hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands and connective tissue of the nose (Rhinophyma)", "Squamous cell carcinoma arising from a chronic actinic keratosis", "Arterial malformation leading to progressive angiomatous swelling"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has phymatous rosacea, classically presenting as Rhinophyma. Rhinophyma is characterized by progressive hyperplasia of the sebaceous glands, blood vessels, and fibrous connective tissue of the nose, occurring almost exclusively in men with long-standing rosacea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #96016: A 68-year-old boy with a history of erythematotelangiectatic rosacea presents with bilateral eye irritation. They complain of a constant ''gritty'' sensation, burning, dryness, and recurrent styes (hordeola). Slit-lamp exam reveals blepharitis, conjunctival injection, and telangiectasias of the eyelid margin. What is the most appropriate systemic treatment?', '["Low-dose oral Doxycycline", "Intravenous Ceftriaxone", "Oral Isotretinoin", "Topical Ganciclovir ophthalmic gel", "Oral Valacyclovir"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The patient has Ocular Rosacea, which affects up to 50% of rosacea patients. It presents with blepharitis, conjunctival injection, a gritty foreign body sensation, and lid margin telangiectasias. While mild cases are managed with eyelid hygiene, moderate-to-severe ocular rosacea requires systemic sub-antimicrobial doxycycline.');
